  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-1, & x\\ne -1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1.$",
  "worked_solution": "For $x=-1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-4.\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\nf(-1)=4\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x).\n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nHence, I is true, and II and III are false.",
